@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/belief#> .
@prefix belief: <http://example.org/orexis/belief#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  OREXIS BELIEF.
#  The belief package's own words: beliefs, and the beliefs derived on top of them. A graph is
#  written as any belief is, and the rules the store holds conclude what follows from it — its
#  revisions — into a graph of its own, derived from it. Any belief is accepted, and revised;
#  nothing refuses one and nothing stands between a writer and the store. A validation and a
#  proposal state to serve it were built here and struck: belief revision keeps the new
#  information, dropping testimony over a shape is a gate wearing revision's name, and a graph
#  with no catalogue row is already invisible to every reader, so a writer that means its graph
#  never to be seen without its revisions writes the graph, revises it and classifies it last.
#  The rules are SHACL 1.2 Inference Rules (W3C FPWD, 2026-09-17), adopted as they stand and
#  nothing added: a graph in the role of a rules graph is typed sh:RulesGraph, a package's rules
#  are a sh:RuleSet with sh:hasRule and sh:includesRuleSet, a rule is a sh:SPARQLRule with
#  sh:construct, placed by sh:layer and sh:order, taken out by sh:deactivated, run once by
#  sh:runOnce; `revise` runs a rules graph's default rule set — every global rule in it, one not
#  linked to a shape by sh:rule — layer by layer to a fixpoint, rules of one order not seeing each
#  other's inferences within an iteration, and keeps the inferences in an inference graph apart
#  from the base graph, never deleting. The draft names that graph as a role and not a class, so
#  the one word here is the class of it. The engine is the store's own, not SHACL's. Classes only:
#  which graph is an agent's is genesis's to create and the catalogue's to say.
#
#  AND A TRANSITION, WHICH IS NOT AN INFERENCE (knowledge/domain/belief/transition.md,
#  a-transition-changes-the-state-and-an-inference-only-concludes). A rule that deletes as well as
#  inserts — a sh:SPARQLRule whose sh:construct is what it adds and whose :delete is a `DELETE …
#  WHERE` naming no graph, grouped by sh:order — changes a state rather than concluding of it, and
#  this package's machine runs it: planning, above, applies an action's effect through it in a
#  possible world; and a rule typed :Transition is applied once to the agent's own state by every
#  arrival of testimony, after the arrival's revisions settle, its WHERE saying what it is about. A
#  transition declares no trigger and no target: where it reads and writes is the runner's, which
#  prepares the target — a state graph of the arrival's own, the kernel's orexis:StateGraph, holding
#  over the arrival's period — so no word here names either. What it deletes is taken out of
#  whichever of the agent's own derived state graphs holds it. `revise` passes over a :Transition,
#  since a transition is run once and never to a fixpoint.
#
#  ONE AXIOM ON THE DRAFT'S TERM, AND NO WORD: a rules graph is a graph. A boot reads a document
#  only where the vocabulary puts its kind beneath `orexis:Graph` — a kind says who reads the
#  document (a-documents-kind-says-who-reads-it) — and this package is the reader of rules graphs,
#  so it is the one to say so. Without it every package's and domain's rules were passed over.

sh:RulesGraph rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph .

:RevisionGraph a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Revision graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """The revisions of ONE source graph — what the rules conclude of it, and nothing
    the source itself says; SHACL 1.2's inference graph, for one source. OF ITS SOURCE'S KIND, said
    beside this one on its row: a percept's revisions are an `orexis:PerceptGraph`, since what the
    rules conclude of what a sensor said is still what it said and no reader of the mind is handed
    it, and any other source's an `orexis:BeliefGraph` (#944). prov:wasDerivedFrom
    the source, orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived, the source's owner and period copied where its
    row states them. Apart from the source because a conclusion is a function of its premises —
    two sources with the same facts have the same conclusions, so a world is hashed by its own
    facts and never by these — and because deletion is never a rule's: the graph is dropped
    whole and rebuilt when the source is rewritten, and goes when the source goes. A reader
    hands it to a rule beside its source; nothing forks it, nothing hashes it, nothing deletes
    from it.""" .

:settled a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :RevisionGraph ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "settled" ;
    rdfs:comment """Whether the rules reached a fixpoint over this graph's source, and the transitions
    its arrival triggers have run. `revise` spends a BUDGET of rule executions per call, the unit
    revision spends, and a source the budget cuts short keeps what was concluded with this false on
    its row; the deliberator says it false too while the source's transitions wait or were cut, and
    continues it on the next pass, and at boot re-queues every row that says false, so a cut
    survives a restart and a rule set that never settles is reported every pass rather than looped
    on.""" .

:delete a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sh:SPARQLRule ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "delete" ;
    rdfs:comment """On a sh:SPARQLRule that changes a state, where SHACL has only sh:construct: a
    `DELETE … WHERE` taking out whatever stands in the place the rule changes, which nobody can name
    in advance. It names no graph: its WHERE is matched over what the runner hands, with every rule
    of its order reading the same state, and what it matches is taken out of the graphs the runner
    says — the world a step makes, for an action's effect; the agent's own derived state graphs,
    for a transition. Applied before the order's additions (knowledge/domain/belief/transition.md).""" .

:Transition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sh:SPARQLRule ;
    rdfs:label "Transition" ;
    rdfs:comment """A SPARQL rule that changes a state rather than concluding of it: its sh:construct
    what it inserts, its :delete what it takes out, grouped by sh:order. Triggered once by each
    arrival of testimony — a graph received or heard — as a database trigger runs once on each row
    inserted, after the arrival's revisions settle; the agent's own graphs, derived or recorded, and
    what the sovereign asserted trigger none, so its own output never triggers it again. It declares no trigger: it
    reads the arrival with its revisions, the public graphs and the agent's own derived state, so its
    WHERE says what it is about, and on an arrival it is not about it matches nothing and changes
    nothing. What it inserts goes into the state graph the runner prepares for the arrival, an
    orexis:StateGraph the arrival's owner's and derived, holding over its period. This type is how
    the runner finds it, and how `revise` and planning's bridge reader pass over it
    (knowledge/domain/belief/transition.md).""" .

:budget a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Self ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "budget" ;
    rdfs:comment """How many rule executions one pass of revision may spend, across every source it
    revises; a source the budget cuts short is continued by the next pass. A stance: the agent
    states it of itself in its self graph and the deliberator reads it when it is made; where it
    states none, two hundred and fifty-six. One source's share of a pass stays `revise`'s own
    constant, which says how a pass's budget is spent and not how much.""" .

#  THE ROLE THIS PACKAGE SERVES (knowledge/domain/belief/deliberator.md). An agent declared a
#  deliberator — in its self graph, or by a role a domain puts beneath this one — runs the
#  deliberator, which revises what it believes by the rules it holds; the boot loads this package
#  for it and for nothing else. It needs nothing of the world: an agent holding no rules graph may
#  still be declared one, and revises nothing.

:Deliberator a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Deliberator" ;
    rdfs:comment """An agent that revises what it believes by the rules it holds: the role whose
    part is the deliberator, one concept with it. Declared, never derived; a role beneath this
    one — the observer's, a market host's — loads this package with its own.""" .
